{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"So the full i915_pm_ops has these entries. Only `.suspend` and `.poweroff` map to i915_pm_suspend. All others map to distinct functions. So the PCI PM callbacks reaching i915_pm_suspend are: .suspend and .poweroff.\n\nNow, are there other i915_pm_ops-like structures? Like a runtime struct or a separate dev_pm_ops? The only dev_pm_ops in i915_driver.c is i915_pm_ops. And the PCI driver's .pm = &i915_pm_ops. So those 16 callbacks are \"every PCI PM callback\".\n\nNow which of the callers (.suspend, .poweroff → both = i915_pm_suspend) also call i915_pm_runtime_suspend? The i915_pm_suspend function body: calls i915_drm_suspend and i915_drm_suspend_late. Does it call i915_pm_runtime_suspend? No (not in i915_driver.c at all). So neither calls i915_pm_runtime_suspend.\n\nBut wait — is i915_pm_runtime_suspend the runtime PM callback, and does the system suspend path invoke runtime suspend? No. The question is literal: \"which of those callers also call i915_pm_runtime_suspend\". Answer: none of them do, because i915_pm_runtime_suspend is the runtime-PM (rpm) suspend callback (registered via the driver's runtime PM ops), a separate path not invoked from the system-suspend i915_pm_suspend. \n\nLet me verify i915_pm_runtime_suspend truly is a separate runtime PM callback and find its location to be accurate. Let me try to find it. Let me grep the runtime pm in the display dir. Actually, let me check if the pci driver also has a separate .runtime / i915_runtime_pm. In i915, runtime PM ops are assigned in the struct. Let me search the whole i915 for it — but directory grep fails. \n\nLet me try to find the file by grepping \"pm_runtime_set_autosuspend\" or \"rpm\" in i915 files I can guess. Let me check display/intel_display_power.c for \"kdev\" runtime callback. Actually, let me search \"i915_pm_runtime_suspend\" once more in display files that host runtime: there might be a dedicated file. Let me check the list of display files by grepping a header include. \n\nActually, I'll accept: i915_pm_runtime_suspend is the rpm callback, defined in the runtime-PM implementation file (intel_runtime_pm). The precise file isn't essential; what matters for the answer is that the system-suspend PCI PM callbacks (.suspend/.poweroff → i915_pm_suspend) do NOT call i915_pm_runtime_suspend.\n\nLet me do one more check to be safe: does i915_drm_suspend or i915_drm_suspend_late (the chain) call i915_pm_runtime_suspend? Not in i915_driver.c. Could it be deeper (i915_gem_suspend → ...)? The question asks about \"those callers\" = the callers reaching i915_pm_suspend = the pm callbacks. It's about direct relationship at the PM-callback level. The answer is none.\n\nActually, re-reading: \"list which of those callers also call i915_pm_runtime_suspend\". \"those callers\" = the PCI PM callbacks that reach i915_pm_suspend. If none, I say none. But let me be thorough and confirm the runtime pm callback name/location. Let me try to find it via the runtime ops. Let me grep \".runtime\" in i915 files. Actually let me just try to find the runtime file: grep \"i915_pm_runtime_suspend\" in drivers/gpu/drm/i915/gt/intel_gt_pm? Let me get gt file listing by grepping \"include \\\"intel_gt.h\\\"\" won't help.\n\nLet me try: the runtime PM in recent i915 is in drivers/gpu/drm/i915/gt/intel_gt_pm.c? No such file. Let me check gt dir with a token. Let me grep \"i915_gem_request_create\" to find gt/gem files, no.\n\nI'll make a final attempt to locate by trying drivers/gpu/drm/i915/i915_gpu_state.c and drivers/gpu/drm/i915/gt/intel_gt_pm (already). Let me try display/intel_power.c.\n\nActually, I think it's better to just note the finding without an exact file, or find it. Let me try one: grep \"i915_pm_runtime_suspend\" in the file that likely holds runtime: given intel_runtime_pm_init_early is called with &dev_priv->runtime_pm, the runtime pm functions (intel_runtime_pm_*) and i915_pm_runtime_* are probably in one file. Let me grep \"i915_pm_runtime\" in display/intel_display_power.c gave nothing. Hmm.\n\nLet me look up where intel_runtime_pm_init_early is defined. Let me grep display dir files. Let me grep \"intel_runtime_pm_init_early\" in display/intel_display.c. Actually let me just grep the include for the struct intel_runtime_pm file. \n\nI'll do a couple more targeted attempts then conclude."}}
