{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"The runtime pm callbacks must be in a .c file under gt. Let me find the gt .c files. Let me grep the header that's included by i915 for i915_pm_runtime. Actually, let me just find the file by trying gt/intel_gt_pm.c variants: maybe it's gt/intel_gt_pm.c but I need the exact. Let me check if the file exists by grepping a known symbol in it. Let me grep \"intel_gt_pm\" symbol... Let me grep the header include. The gt/intel_gt_pm.c — grep returned \"No such file\", so the file really doesn't have that name. \n\nLet me find which .c file has i915_pm_runtime_suspend by checking the .pm assignment of the pci driver, which is elsewhere. Let me grep the whole kernel for i915_pm_runtime usage in the pci file. The i915 pci_driver is probably in i915_driver.c under a different grep. Let me grep \"i915_driver\" struct. Actually, drm_pci_driver. Let me grep \"drm_pci_driver\" or \".driver\".\n\nI think I've gathered enough for a solid answer. The key facts:\n1. i915_pm_ops in drivers/gpu/drm/i915/i915_driver.c is the PCI PM ops. 16 callbacks. Only .suspend and .poweroff point to i915_pm_suspend.\n2. i915_pm_suspend body: kdev_to_i915 → i915_drm_suspend(&i915->drm) and (in the same function) i915_drm_suspend_late. It does NOT call i915_pm_runtime_suspend.\n3. i915_pm_runtime_suspend is the runtime PM (rpm) callback — a separate subsystem path registered as the driver's runtime PM ops, defined outside i915_driver.c (in the intel runtime-PM implementation). The system-suspend PCI PM callbacks do not invoke it.\n\nLet me try one more to nail the runtime file, grepping the gt files. Let me list gt files by grepping \"intel_gt_pm\" header include references. Actually let me grep \"i915_pm_runtime_suspend\" in gt/intel_gt.c — did (no). gt/intel_device.c — \"no such file\". Let me find gt file names by grepping the Kbuild or by grep \"include \\\"intel_gt_pm.h\\\"\". Let me grep that include to see which .c include it."}}
